From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 09:13:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: an endless poll is endless, not a hundred-millisecond
 timeout of our own
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Nextendo's own list has this title verified and playing online on Ryujinx-Nextendo, so the client's
TLS does run there and the difference is in this host, not in the game or the server. Two of our
socket answers differ from the reference service, and both live around the handshake:

The reference blocks in select/poll until an fd is ready. This host answered every `poll(-1)` with 0
after 100 ms — log 5640DC4F has seven of them in a row before the server's flight arrived — so an
endless wait reads to the title as a timeout it never asked for. The slice stays, but it is now only
how often the teardown hook is re-read: with `ms < 0` both paths keep waiting instead of returning.

The other is `DM_NEXTENDO_READ_WAIT_MS`, which holds a non-blocking read for 250 ms before answering
EAGAIN; the reference answers at once, and `0` is that answer — an env change, no build.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: the Nextendo online seams are this fork's own work: upstream's multi-worktree tree takes them to seedds/nxnative instead
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)

---
 dm/host/hle_net.c | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_net.c b/dm/host/hle_net.c
index 832a3b7c..45f6053a 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_net.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_net.c
@@ -1233,10 +1233,14 @@ static int sk_poll_local(void *fds, uint64_t n, int ms) {
     for (uint64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) if (p[i].fd >= 0 && sk_used(p[i].fd)) { open |= 1ull << p[i].fd; generation[p[i].fd] = g_efd[p[i].fd].generation; }
     for (uint64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i].revents = 0;
     if (!open) { pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m); return sk_idle(ms); }
-    // Periodically return from an endless wait, as before, so guest garbage collection/shutdown can observe a thread outside the host hook.
-    int64_t limit = (ms < 0 ? 100 : (int64_t)ms) * 1000000; uint64_t start = guest_ns(); int ready;
+    // An endless wait is endless: the slice is how often the teardown hook is re-read, not a timeout the caller
+    // asked for. Returning 0 from `poll(-1)` made every endless wait look like a timeout of the caller's own
+    // (log 5640DC4F: seven `poll(-1) -> 0` in a row before the server's flight arrived), which the reference
+    // service never does — it blocks in select/poll until an fd is ready.
+    int64_t limit = (ms < 0 ? -1 : (int64_t)ms * 1000000); uint64_t start = guest_ns(); int ready;
     while (!(ready = efd_ready(p, n, open, generation))) {
         if (dm_teardown_is_parking()) { pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m); dm_teardown_check_current_thread(); return 0; }
+        if (limit < 0) { dm_guest_cond_wait_ns(&g_efd_c, &g_efd_m, 1000000); continue; }
         int64_t left = limit - (int64_t)(guest_ns() - start); if (left <= 0) break;
         dm_guest_cond_wait_ns(&g_efd_c, &g_efd_m, dm_detclock_enabled() && left > 1000000 ? 1000000 : left);
     }
@@ -1246,7 +1250,7 @@ static int sk_poll_local(void *fds, uint64_t n, int ms) {
 // sockets by the host's poll, and the loop sleeps a millisecond between samples. PRUDP is a network
 // wait, not a hot path, so a millisecond of granularity costs nothing and keeps the two locks apart.
 static int nd_poll_mixed(sk_pollfd *p, uint64_t n, int ms) {
-    int64_t start = guest_ns(), limit = (ms < 0 ? 100 : (int64_t)ms) * 1000000;
+    int64_t start = guest_ns(), limit = (ms < 0 ? -1 : (int64_t)ms * 1000000);
     for (;;) {
         int ready = 0;
         sk_pollfd local[EFD_MAX]; int idx[EFD_MAX]; int nl = 0;
@@ -1262,6 +1266,11 @@ static int nd_poll_mixed(sk_pollfd *p, uint64_t n, int ms) {
             for (int k = 0; k < nl; k++) if (local[k].revents) { p[idx[k]].revents = local[k].revents; ready++; }
         }
         if (ready) return ready;
+        if (limit < 0) {   // endless: wait, do not answer a timeout of our own (see the datagram path above)
+            if (dm_teardown_is_parking()) { dm_teardown_check_current_thread(); return 0; }
+            dm_guest_sleep_ns(1000000);
+            continue;
+        }
         int64_t left = limit - (int64_t)(guest_ns() - start);
         if (left <= 0) return 0;
         dm_guest_sleep_ns(left > 1000000 ? 1000000 : left);
